    "result_text": "The build succeeded with the full suite green (`AcceptanceBase` is abstract and `AuditLogCapture` is a helper, so `AcceptanceTest` is the only executable test \u2014 it passes).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `capacityWarning` flag following the existing `bulkSignupWarning` pattern (a persisted, fixed-at-creation flag, since it's read back via `GET` and the response mapper has no repository access).\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `capacityWarning` field (`@Column(name = \"capacity_warning\")`) with getter/setter.\n\n2. **`AssignCapacityWarning.java`** (new) \u2014 runs after `BuildOwner`, before `SaveOwner`. Counts existing owners in the same city using the identical case-insensitive/whitespace-collapsed normalization as `CheckOwnerCityCapacity`, then sets `capacityWarning = count >= 40 && count < 50`. Since the hard rejection at 50 already ran in `CheckOwnerCityCapacity`, the count is never \u226550 here.\n\n3. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired `assignCapacityWarning` into the create pipeline between `assignPossibleDuplicate` and `save`, under `govern: [ transaction ]` like the other steps.\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `capacityWarning` boolean property to the `OwnerDto` schema, so the generated DTO carries it and MapStruct maps it by name.\n\n5. **Schema DDL** (`h2`, `hsqldb`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 added the `capacity_warning BOOLEAN` column.\n\nThe hard rejection at 50 in `CheckOwnerCityCapacity` is unchanged. The acceptance test (`fillCity` to 40 \u2192 next owner warns) passes.",
